	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0033355533&doi=10.1080%2f02286203.1999.11760435&partnerID=40&md5=25f25c51c5c52f38e2887299457e733b},
	affiliations = {Dept. of Electron. and Elec. Eng., Loughborough University, Leicestershire LE11 3TU, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Previous analyses of the surfaces of machined components have failed to identify a common textural form and consequently a complete characterization of a significant group of such surfaces has not been produced. In this paper, the reason for this failure is shown to be the current standard practice of confining analysis to the shorter wavelengths of the texture. The new method is an analysis of not only the shorter wavelengths, but also of the longer texture wavelengths. To achieve this, an extended Fourier analysis method was developed and from the common textural form identified, a complete description is devised involving only two parameters, both of which are defined so as to be easily understood in terms of present surface texture analysis parameters. The first parameter which can be estimated is the root-mean-square value of a profile of unlimited length. The second parameter, termed the `surface cut-off wavelength' is shown to be the automatically calculated value of the cut-off wavelength normally estimated by the instrument operator and selected on the measurement instrument. Hence, both the expertise and the uncertainty involved in the selection of the cut-off wavelength are eliminated. The new analysis is shown to provide a complete description of the surface texture of 144 specimen surfaces produced by a wide variety of machining processes. The investigation is extended to the characterization of the surface textures of components subjected to wear, and these results show that the analysis is equally applicable to the description of surfaces both of specimens which have been subjected to crossed cylinder abrasive wear, and of specimens subjected to pin-on-disc wear.},
	keywords = {Machining; Parameter estimation; Surface structure; Textures; Wear of materials; Signal identification; Surface texture analysis; Signal processing},
